{
"An act is called unskillful when it comes from greed, hatred, or confusion, and it is recognized by the harm it leads to.": "308ff7887207",
"Aristotle holds that the good for a human being is a life of excellent activity, that the excellences are settled traits acquired by practice, and that a person of practical wisdom sees what to do without consulting a rule. Each point has a Buddhist counterpart. The path is a training. The precepts are called training rules, and one undertakes them as one takes up an exercise. Feeling is to be educated and not merely overruled, so that the trained person no longer wants to harm. And the tradition has its own word for the trained perception of what a situation calls for: skill. Wholesome acts are kusala, skillful, the word used for a good craftsman.": "8e2bccab3844",
"Aristotle is the closest. Buddhist ethics and his share a shape: a conception of the best condition of a person, an account of the traits that make it up, a method of acquiring them by practice, and a trust in trained judgment over formula. Utilitarianism shares the Buddhas motive and his reach, and it captures something real in the Mahāyāna. Kant shares the least.": "5481375e91f0",
"Before you act, the Buddha told his son, look at what you are about to do as you would look in a mirror, and ask whether it will hurt you or anyone else.": "1f871716d88a",
"English moral vocabulary was shaped by Christianity, and several of its commonest words bring assumptions the Dharma does not make. Commandment implies someone who commands. The precepts are undertakings, and the formula for each is I undertake the training rule to refrain from Sin implies an offense against God that calls for forgiveness. The Buddhist terms are unwholesome or unskillful, which describe an act by its roots and its results, as one would describe bad carpentry. Good and evil suggests two powers at war, and skillful and unskillful suggests competence, which is nearer the mark. Karma is heard as fate or as cosmic reward and punishment. It means action, and the teaching is that intentional acts shape the one who does them, with no judge involved. Merit can sound like a bank balance, and is better introduced as the strength that good habits build.": "13d0014d2eb1",
"Ethics comes first on the path for a practical reason: a mind full of regret cannot become calm.": "e5903182aed0",
"In Buddhism kindness is a skill. You practice it, and you get better at it.": "5731678be762",
"In every Western theory ethics is a subject complete in itself. In the Buddhas teaching it is the first third of a path. Ethical conduct comes first because a mind troubled by remorse cannot settle. Concentration is built on that calm, and wisdom on concentration. Then the order turns back on itself. The person who sees, as the previous pages in this series described, that there is no fixed owner of experience has lost the main reason anyone has for putting himself first. Kindness becomes easier because the wall it had to climb is lower. The tradition accordingly treats ethics both as the precondition of insight and as its natural expression, and it has practical methods for each stage: the precepts, the meditations on loving-kindness and compassion, and the analysis of the self.": "51036fb092ff",
"Karma means action. What you do on purpose shapes what you become. Nobody is keeping score.": "6feeb28c7e3f",
"The Buddha did not give commandments. He gave training rules, which a person takes up the way one takes up an exercise.": "1942982be142",
"The Buddha taught ethics constantly and never offered a theory of it. He did not ask what makes right acts right. He asked what leads to suffering and what leads to its end. Still, when the discourses give reasons for acting one way and not another, the reasons are of three kinds, and they are the three kinds this series met in the lesson on right action.": "df8bf816b2ea",
"The first is consequence. The Buddha tells his son Rāhula to reflect on every act of body, speech, and mind as one looks into a mirror: Does this act with the body that I want to do lead to hurting myself, hurting others, or hurting both? If it does, it is not to be done (Majjhima Nikāya 61). To the Kālāmas, who were confused by rival teachers, he gives a similar standard. They should not go by tradition, scripture, or logic alone. But when you know for yourselves: These things are unskillful, blameworthy, criticized by sensible people, and when you undertake them, they lead to harm and suffering, then you should give them up (Aṅguttara Nikāya 3.65).": "2985e14b5c78",
"The likeness to Aristotle ends at four places. First, the self. Aristotles ethics is about my flourishing, and the virtues are good because they make my life go well. The Buddha teaches that the self whose life this is cannot be found, and the end of the path is described as the end of clinging to it. Second, scope. Aristotles virtues are those of a free male citizen of a Greek city. The Buddhas concern extends to slaves, women, animals, and enemies. Third, the list. Aristotle counts proper pride and well-aimed anger as virtues, and the Buddha counts neither. He admits no right amount of hatred. Compassion and non-attachment, which are central for the Buddha, are not on Aristotles list at all. Fourth, the setting. For the Buddha acts have results that follow the agent beyond this life, by a law that no one administers. Aristotles ethics is complete within one lifetime.": "98f46b39f289",
"The precepts look like Kantian duties, and both teachers hold that every person counts and that one must not make an exception of oneself. The resemblance does not go deep. Kants moral law is a command of reason, binding whatever one feels, and an act has moral worth only when done from duty. The Buddha issues no commands. A precept is a resolution that a person makes because he sees where its breach would lead, and its worth lies in the state of mind it expresses. Kant distrusts inclination. The Buddhist path aims to transform it. And Kants ethics rests on the autonomy of a rational self, the very thing the teaching of not-self takes apart.": "b64a380080b6",
"The second is rule. Lay followers undertake five precepts: to refrain from killing, stealing, sexual misconduct, lying, and intoxicants. Monks and nuns keep several hundred. The third is character. The same discourse to the Kālāmas goes on to say why those things lead to harm. A person overcome by greed, hatred, or delusion kills, steals, and lies. These three are called the unwholesome roots, and an act is unwholesome because it grows from them (Aṅguttara Nikāya 3.69). The best-known summary of the teaching puts the third strand last, where it gathers up the other two: Not to do any evil; to embrace the good; to purify ones mind: this is the instruction of the Buddhas (Dhammapada 183).": "ecf44b7c550a",
"This is why some scholars, Jay Garfield among them, say that the debate over labels misses the point. Buddhist ethics, on their reading, is chiefly concerned with changing how a person perceives: seeing others as fellow sufferers and oneself as one being among many. Right action follows from right seeing. Whether or not that is the whole story, it explains why the Buddha could teach ethics for forty-five years without once stating a criterion of right action.": "d0209b8b730c",
"Utilitarianism shares the Buddhas starting point, that suffering is bad wherever it occurs, and his scope, which is every being that can feel. The Mahāyāna comes closest. The bodhisattva vows to work for the liberation of all beings, and the eighth-century teacher Śāntideva argues, as a utilitarian would, that my suffering has no special claim because it is mine: suffering is to be removed simply because it is suffering. Stories in which a bodhisattva breaks a precept to prevent greater harm point the same way. What is missing is the sum. No Buddhist text adds the happiness of some to offset the misery of others, and the act that breaks a rule is judged by the purity of the compassion behind it.": "ecbbab46abd9"
}
